.TH TROPICCTL 1 "" "tropicctl 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tropicctl \- command-line tool for the TROPIC01 secure element
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tropicctl
[global options] <area> <verb> [arguments]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generated from the command table; see also the reference in the source tree.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-spi\-dev PATH
SPI device (default /dev/spidev2.0, or $TROPICCTL_SPI_DEV)
.TP
.B \-\-gpio\-dev PATH
GPIO chip (default /dev/gpiochip0)
.TP
.B \-\-cs\-pin N
chip\-select GPIO line (default 25)
.TP
.B \-\-spi\-speed HZ
SPI clock (default 1000000)
.TP
.B \-\-key SOURCE
pairing key: auto (default), shared (the slot\-0 key set by TROPICCTL_SH0 or <key dir>/slot0\-key: es01 or prod0), es01, prod0, unit, file:PATH:SLOT, pin
.TP
.B \-\-pin\-file FILE, \-\-pin\-stdin
where a PIN comes from (never the command line); otherwise a terminal prompt
.TP
.B \-\-json
machine\-readable output on stdout; errors as a JSON object on stderr
.TP
.B \-q, \-v
quiet / verbose notices on stderr
.TP
.B \-\-lock\-timeout SEC
how long to wait for the chip lock (default 10)
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
check everything read\-only, say what would be written, write nothing
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lock
do not take the chip lock (tests and debugging only)
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-h
print the version / this help
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B chip reboot

.br
[W] Restart the chip. Restarts the chip so that a rewritten R\-config is loaded. The chip loads its R\-config at start\-up: after config rewrite or config restore the new access rights are not in force until this (or a power cycle). Ends any session; no key needed.
.TP
.B fw status

.br
[R] Firmware versions, silicon revision and mode. Prints the RISC\-V and SPECT firmware versions, the chip's silicon revision, whether this build's update images fit it, and the chip mode. No secure session is needed.
.TP
.B fw update
(\-\-dir DIR | \-\-cpu FILE \-\-spect FILE) \-\-yes\-update\-firmware [\-\-enable\-maintenance] [\-\-disable\-maintenance] [\-\-backup FILE]
.br
[I] Update the mutable firmware (both banks). IRREVERSIBLE: a chip never accepts an older version, and an interrupted or wrong update can leave it unusable. Takes the signed images as libtropic ships them (TROPIC01_fw_update_files/boot_v_X/fw_v_X_Y_Z; the versions are read from the file names). Refuses images for another silicon revision, a downgrade, and a chip whose I\-config has Maintenance Mode disabled. \-\-enable\-maintenance enables it in the R\-config first (backup, erase, write, restart); \-\-disable\-maintenance turns it off again after the update has been read back. Run it from a stable power supply and do not interrupt it.
.TP
.B fw maintenance
<on|off> \-\-yes\-rewrite\-r\-config [\-\-backup FILE]
.br
[H] Turn Maintenance Mode on or off in the R\-config. Rewrites the R\-config START_UP object (backup, erase, write, read back, restart). Maintenance Mode is what a firmware update needs; leaving it on enlarges the attack surface.
.TP
.B info chip

.br
[R] Print the chip identification. Reads CHIP_ID. No secure session is needed. The text is the layout `lt\-util \-i` printed; with \-\-json every CHIP_ID group is a hex string, as libtropic defines the groups, without interpretation.
.TP
.B info fw

.br
[R] Print the RISC\-V and SPECT firmware version bytes. Prints the version bytes as the chip reports them and the version they stand for, read as libtropic reads them (byte 3 . byte 2 . byte 1; byte 0 is not part of the version). No secure session is needed.
.TP
.B info mode

.br
[R] Print the chip mode (application or maintenance). No secure session is needed. Most commands need the chip in application mode.
.TP
.B info cert
[\-\-out\-dir DIR]
.br
[R] List the chip's certificate store. Prints the length of each of the four store certificates; with \-\-out\-dir each is written as certN.der. Verifying the chain against Tropic Square's root is not done by this command. No secure session is needed.
.TP
.B rng get
<count> [\-\-out FILE|\-] [\-\-hex]
.br
[R] Random bytes from the chip (1 to 255). Without \-\-out the bytes are printed as hex. With \-\-out FILE (or \- for stdout) they are written raw. Needs a secure session.
.TP
.B mem list
[\-\-range A\-B]
.br
[R] Which R\-MEM slots hold data, and how much. Reads each slot in the range (default 0\-511; every slot is one chip command, so the full range takes a while) and lists the populated ones.
.TP
.B mem read
<slot> [\-\-out FILE|\-]
.br
[R] Read an R\-MEM slot (0\-511). Without \-\-out the content is printed as hex; with \-\-out it is written raw. An empty slot is exit code 6.
.TP
.B mem write
<slot> \-\-in FILE|\- [\-\-replace]
.br
[W] Write an R\-MEM slot (1 to 475 bytes). Refuses an occupied slot unless \-\-replace is given (then the slot is erased first). The slot is read back and compared; a mismatch is exit code 8. With \-\-dry\-run nothing is written.
.TP
.B mem erase
<slot>
.br
[W] Erase an R\-MEM slot. Erases the slot and checks that it reads empty. Erasing an empty slot is not an error. With \-\-dry\-run nothing is written.
.TP
.B ecc list

.br
[R] The 32 ECC key slots: empty, or curve and origin. Origin is generated (made inside the chip, never exported) or stored (a key that was loaded from outside, which the chip cannot vouch for).
.TP
.B ecc generate
<slot> \-\-curve p256|ed25519 [\-\-replace \-\-yes\-erase\-ecc\-key]
.br
[W] Generate a key inside the chip. The private key never leaves the chip. An occupied slot is refused; \-\-replace erases it first and needs \-\-yes\-erase\-ecc\-key, because the old key is lost for good. The key is read back and checked.
.TP
.B ecc store
<slot> \-\-curve p256|ed25519 \-\-in FILE [\-\-replace \-\-yes\-erase\-ecc\-key]
.br
[W] Store a private key from a file. The file holds the 32\-byte private key. The key's origin is then "stored", not "generated", which matters for trust. Rules for an occupied slot as for generate.
.TP
.B ecc pub
<slot> [\-\-out FILE|\-] [\-\-format raw|pem]
.br
[R] The public key of a slot. raw is 64 bytes X||Y for P\-256 and 32 bytes for Ed25519; pem is a SubjectPublicKeyInfo. Without \-\-out it is printed.
.TP
.B ecc erase
<slot> \-\-yes\-erase\-ecc\-key
.br
[I] Erase an ECC key. The key is destroyed for good (a generated key cannot be recreated). Needs \-\-yes\-erase\-ecc\-key; checks the slot reads empty afterwards. Erasing an empty slot is not an error.
.TP
.B ecc sign
<slot> \-\-in FILE [\-\-out FILE|\-] [\-\-alg ecdsa|eddsa] [\-\-digest] [\-\-der]
.br
[R] Sign with a key in the chip. ECDSA signs a 32\-byte hash: without \-\-digest the input is hashed with SHA\-256, with \-\-digest it must be exactly 32 bytes. EdDSA signs the message (up to 4095 bytes). The algorithm follows the slot's curve; a mismatch is refused. The signature is 64 raw bytes (r||s), or DER with \-\-der (ECDSA).
.TP
.B pairing list

.br
[R] State of the four pairing slots. Each slot is populated, empty or invalidated. Reading the slots needs a session; with the shared key this works unless the chip's access rights say otherwise.
.TP
.B pairing read
<slot> [\-\-out FILE]
.br
[R] Read the public pairing key of a slot (0\-3). Prints the 32\-byte public key as hex, or writes it raw with \-\-out. An empty or invalidated slot is exit code 6.
.TP
.B pairing write
<slot> \-\-in FILE \-\-yes\-write\-pairing\-slot
.br
[I] Write a public pairing key into an empty slot (1\-3). IRREVERSIBLE: a pairing slot is written once. The file holds the 32\-byte public key. Slot 0 (the shared engineering key) is refused. The slot must read empty first; it is read back and compared afterwards, because older firmware can fail silently. With \-\-dry\-run the checks run and nothing is written.
.TP
.B pairing invalidate
<slot> \-\-yes\-invalidate\-pairing\-slot [\-\-yes\-invalidate\-shared\-key]
.br
[I] Invalidate a pairing slot. IRREVERSIBLE. The slot being invalidated must not be the key that opened this session. Slot 0 also needs \-\-yes\-invalidate\-shared\-key and a session opened with another, working pairing key, so that the chip stays reachable.
.TP
.B config dump
[\-\-decode]
.br
[R] Every R\-config and I\-config object as raw words. Prints "R NAME 0xVALUE" lines for the reconfigurable objects and "I NAME 0xVALUE" lines for the one\-way objects. \-\-decode adds, for each access object, which pairing keys may use the command on which group of targets.
.TP
.B config get
<name>
.br
[R] One configuration object (the TR01_CFG_ prefix may be left out). Prints the R\-config and the I\-config word of the object.
.TP
.B config diff
\-\-in FILE [\-\-check]
.br
[R] Compare the chip's R\-config with a file. Lists each differing object with which pairing keys would gain or lose access. With \-\-check the exit code is 8 if anything differs.
.TP
.B config restrict-shared
\-\-unit\-slot N [\-\-out FILE|\-]
.br
[R] Build a config file that takes the shared key's rights away. Reads the chip's current R\-config and writes a copy with pairing key 0 (the shared engineering key) removed from every access object except PING. Refuses unless pairing slot N holds a key and has the right to read and rewrite the configuration, so the restriction can be undone. Changes nothing on the chip; apply the file with config rewrite.
.TP
.B config deny
\-\-in FILE \-\-out FILE \-\-pairing\-key K <object[,object...]|all>...
.br
[R] Remove a pairing key from access objects in a config file. Offline: copies a configuration file and removes pairing key K from the named access objects (for example MCOUNTER_INIT,R_CONFIG_WRITE_ERASE), in every group of targets; all names every access object. Prints each change. Nothing on the chip changes; apply the file with config rewrite, which refuses a file that locks the session's key out of the configuration.
.TP
.B config rewrite
\-\-in FILE \-\-yes\-rewrite\-r\-config [\-\-backup FILE] [\-\-include\-non\-access]
.br
[H] Rewrite the R\-config from a file. HAZARDOUS. Validates the file, saves the current R\-config to a backup, ERASES then writes the R\-config, and reads it back. Writing without erasing can put the chip into permanent Alarm Mode, so this is the only way the tool writes it. Refuses a file that would take the session key's right to read or rewrite the configuration, and a file that changes objects that are not access rights unless \-\-include\-non\-access is given. If the write fails after the erase the message says so and names the backup.
.TP
.B config restore
\-\-in BACKUP \-\-yes\-rewrite\-r\-config
.br
[H] Write a saved R\-config back. The same safe sequence as rewrite, for a backup file that config rewrite saved.
.TP
.B config burn
<name> <bit> \-\-yes\-burn\-i\-config\-bit
.br
[I] Clear one I\-config bit (one\-way). IRREVERSIBLE: an I\-config bit only goes from 1 to 0. Refused unless the CPU firmware is 2.0.0 or newer (older firmware can fail the write silently); the bit is read back afterwards.
.TP
.B mnd setup
\-\-state FILE \-\-out KEYFILE \-\-yes\-setup\-mnd [\-\-slots N] [\-\-base\-slot B] [\-\-add\-file F] [\-\-replace]
.br
[I] Set a new PIN (MAC\-and\-Destroy). Runs the new\-PIN procedure of Tropic Square's application note ODN_TR01_app_002: initialises N chip slots (default 5) from the base slot, writes the host state (needs no protection) to \-\-state and the released 32\-byte key to \-\-out. The PIN (4 to 8 characters) comes from \-\-pin\-file, \-\-pin\-stdin or a terminal prompt, never the command line.
.TP
.B mnd check
\-\-state FILE \-\-out KEYFILE [\-\-add\-file F]
.br
[W] Check a PIN and release the key. A wrong PIN destroys one chip slot and uses one attempt; when none remain the key can never be released. The decremented attempt count is saved before the chip is touched.
.TP
.B mnd status
\-\-state FILE
.br
[R] Attempts left according to the host state. Reads only the state file (no chip access). The chip's own slots are authoritative.
.TP
.B mnd seal
\-\-key\-file K \-\-in FILE \-\-out FILE
.br
[R] Encrypt a file with a released key (AES\-256\-GCM). For the PIN\-protected pairing key: seals the 32\-byte private key under the key mnd check released. Host only.
.TP
.B mnd unseal
\-\-key\-file K \-\-in FILE \-\-out FILE
.br
[R] Decrypt a sealed file. The reverse of seal. Exit code 8 if the key is wrong or the file is damaged. Host only.
.TP
.B counter get
<n>
.br
[R] Read a monotonic counter (0\-15). An uninitialised counter is exit code 6.
.TP
.B counter init
<n> <value> \-\-yes\-init\-counter
.br
[I] Set a monotonic counter (0 to 4294967294). Per libtropic's functional test a counter can be initialised again, so rollback protection holds only after the chip's access rights remove the init right from every key. Read back afterwards.
.TP
.B counter update
<n> \-\-yes\-update\-counter
.br
[I] Decrement a monotonic counter by one. Per libtropic's functional test an update decrements by one and fails at zero (refused here before it is tried).
.TP
.B session test

.br
[R] Open and close a secure session; say which key was used. Reports the pairing key and slot that opened the session. Exit code 4 if no key works. With \-\-key pin the PIN\-protected key is used (PIN from \-\-pin\-file, \-\-pin\-stdin or the terminal).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
success
.TP
.B 1
internal error (a bug)
.TP
.B 2
usage error (bad arguments)
.TP
.B 3
chip unreachable or busy (SPI, GPIO, lock timeout)
.TP
.B 4
secure session failed (no usable pairing key, key refused)
.TP
.B 5
access denied by the chip's access rights
.TP
.B 6
slot empty or object not found
.TP
.B 7
slot already written
.TP
.B 8
verification failed (read\-back mismatch)
.TP
.B 9
refused by a safety rule (missing confirmation flag, forbidden target)
.TP
.B 10
the chip reported another error
